#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cb0bd2-c4ef-5dd6-8c0b-38f8a14033cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459509} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2641ca-1b88-5d2b-a9c3-627699c70f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2641ca-1b88-5d2b-a9c3-627699c70f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459510} :answer-field/id #uuid "701b7295-ba30-510e-8aed-ce8e567b3964" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460621} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b712a69-6903-5945-beae-57def102aba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459511} {:answer/id #uuid "9c38e779-e7e6-5c6a-b62e-5d5b58d2d870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b712a69-6903-5945-beae-57def102aba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459511} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0c6886-c030-53c3-a1a5-957b02f81b60" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0a984a-de37-5319-9554-273e15f7477f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459513} {:answer/id #uuid "0d197d92-6732-599f-a209-f5a39a673b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd0a984a-de37-5319-9554-273e15f7477f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459513} :answer-field/id #uuid "05075a1e-9b2d-5493-88e7-7c07957ae17d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec1aa6d1-7c31-58d9-aff7-9461af74c578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459515} {:answer/id #uuid "f18b9afa-c071-5b2c-ab57-e1879ab20b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec1aa6d1-7c31-58d9-aff7-9461af74c578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459515} :answer-field/id #uuid "27c273e9-3d77-5c11-8cf8-2b50e06e227b" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "41a70c70-f13c-564f-a1cc-c8837528e651" :question/math-academy-id "q-125636" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d40cf388-ace4-5cff-ada6-1673e5e97d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448200} {:answer/id #uuid "3038d1f6-6212-58a8-b86d-a76d84b57160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3038d1f6-6212-58a8-b86d-a76d84b57160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448201} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe07bb4a-705f-5e32-9710-7321adbcae68" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449140} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df2c42fd-7c23-501b-a763-96fa00dbe8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448202} {:answer/id #uuid "94707d81-43e0-5f96-a07d-b6f0edb7ceec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94707d81-43e0-5f96-a07d-b6f0edb7ceec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448203} :answer-field/id #uuid "673af9ea-69b0-5da6-8e80-899b018d9e08" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c888a3f0-aad5-5214-8bc6-e27333ae2d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448204} {:answer/id #uuid "fa849542-2afc-5697-bb40-d4cdbcae4678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa849542-2afc-5697-bb40-d4cdbcae4678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ca9ca1-ba5a-5998-8516-91b8d08ef6d3" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c64f72-bc4e-5149-99d1-c9f993fca3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125663" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e13203b-40c8-53bb-bf92-d0da61a9b594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256918} {:answer/id #uuid "49ceb4c7-4cd7-5605-a7ee-9d2efe7805c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257426} {:answer/id #uuid "870ebadc-8234-54f2-9d35-fd0226712313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257966} {:answer/id #uuid "b11abca9-a7f3-505b-a3af-181b6dca6940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258324} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bb86bf-0650-5edd-b566-aef27268caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e13203b-40c8-53bb-bf92-d0da61a9b594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256918} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fac6ca7-9e3d-5b0d-a31d-797123e624e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02f61416-bc2b-5dde-8108-fea0cc0be63b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125665" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fa8d02-75d6-5d90-ad84-bf573bb9d9df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415204} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4b9cfd-a831-5fd0-85ab-698650ea4e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415220} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f2143-dc01-5291-af26-417281491c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415239} {:answer/id #uuid "d55a9329-8e18-5108-8eab-de793cc094fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415250} {:answer/id #uuid "da48178e-9646-5e30-ac04-7bbdb41f44dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86f2143-dc01-5291-af26-417281491c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415239} :answer-field/id #uuid "c40c7417-b7c5-57c2-ac5e-693212f63842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be0d9d11-b021-5cdb-982a-0da35bda23a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125727" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(4(1)-3(-3))-(4(0)-3(2)) \\\\  & =4+9+6 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a30b11a-120a-5f7a-8652-df80e513b7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256875} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3dcd67-78eb-583f-97c5-32ac61d09e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258173} {:answer/id #uuid "adfaf3dc-e92b-57ed-a5f8-0dc1710ebeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258300} {:answer/id #uuid "af03ba57-28b4-5704-b56c-cd9a3133f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258307} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1a8fbb-7b74-5316-bf06-61f28d3e5539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af03ba57-28b4-5704-b56c-cd9a3133f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258307} :answer-field/id #uuid "075c178f-e1b3-5fc0-885f-31705c3ec578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25a22695-171f-55bf-b6a5-1f3b602faf0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125760" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8139aeea-718b-5375-803a-93347bbc782c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168688} {:answer/id #uuid "82c8004a-2ac3-5893-a60f-73d6f8e77a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168689} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f9605f-4b60-5e53-b7a3-b64df5b69052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168690} {:answer/id #uuid "1be0a1ca-8486-5941-bb49-31449f0eb29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168691} {:answer/id #uuid "5d79f02e-fcd8-5711-9839-d7029ccbd6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c8004a-2ac3-5893-a60f-73d6f8e77a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168689} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a5efa42-6a58-5189-86a4-80345f8c4752" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f0fa3f4-0fcf-5a8c-888e-2010c07d0c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-126814" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\,3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/269e3c7f92cee66c78f3226e7c967eb51153503c598d2e4d1e4b56a33e646ac4.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3{x}^{5}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=15{x}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{5}+6xy]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(3{y}^{5}+6{y}^{2})-(-3{y}^{5}-6{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{y}^{5}+12{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{6}+4{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+4)-0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cce3c051-8e34-52bf-a328-cf2bae2f1978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168694} {:answer/id #uuid "d07d23e7-c9a1-5474-bc02-6ad49c38d81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168695} {:answer/id #uuid "f347b7c9-09a6-5a1e-9592-47bb2c2f700e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168696} {:answer/id #uuid "566d27a3-e718-52ac-bea9-996abd18cb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168697} {:answer/id #uuid "568b3c00-9ef3-5583-91de-d209a5cc3091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f347b7c9-09a6-5a1e-9592-47bb2c2f700e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168696} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acd108e-91bd-50f6-9a35-23b824bd34f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7800f0b-56af-56c9-9f5d-febc0c902fbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-126817" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\,{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,{|}_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{4}-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e2ed19-98ac-5bcb-a221-0caee13960fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260093} {:answer/id #uuid "337fdb38-6a77-58a2-a8ba-0427000097ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260341} {:answer/id #uuid "bc81f5e4-801a-52a8-8bc4-b60b5b28c7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261536} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c1ff6-71eb-5c98-bf53-9f9db4823148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261667} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0dfe85-b143-550c-becd-1c76a74d08ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c1ff6-71eb-5c98-bf53-9f9db4823148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261667} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d96bed-7e56-5fe7-b897-4e96898d3e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4e547a4-3c18-52fb-b4b9-408da110d4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126820" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d24203b1-5e2d-543b-b875-84082292041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168706} {:answer/id #uuid "12450fac-5904-57a0-92e2-bc8a3468008b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168707} {:answer/id #uuid "23c8d906-57d9-5dfc-98ed-afb30ba1cec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168708} {:answer/id #uuid "58920047-051c-5179-a311-88f110077114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168709} {:answer/id #uuid "5d55495b-d3c0-5a44-b16e-2735230596b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d24203b1-5e2d-543b-b875-84082292041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168706} :answer-field/id #uuid "744dba16-b85d-5809-875d-1211f267487f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceef6c7e-683c-550f-97a1-9156380979d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126821" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/37/3786d0a5fb99d1a32d761e067f9c49b6b7da05a11fc555dd94a4ef42d753f129.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x-{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-1)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6730175f-e2ca-5f18-8300-20f526084f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424462} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5ab6e6-3ed2-5325-ad32-339384996043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424477} {:answer/id #uuid "ae24f312-5321-5cc9-87dd-f8625d1a40c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424483} {:answer/id #uuid "b7765c6a-5ae2-501d-ad46-c25d34ebcac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424484} {:answer/id #uuid "e838f00f-7aa2-51c0-8734-9a7dc0bd6c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(y-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d5ab6e6-3ed2-5325-ad32-339384996043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424477} :answer-field/id #uuid "6725b75c-c66c-52af-8566-302236c9d990" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eeef3f59-4373-5478-9f71-3d8588687de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127070" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+1$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb0875b-4ef8-58eb-8217-9c53fb18ec9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260529} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8d79b2-c27e-5bcf-9329-f7f7ae12d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261120} {:answer/id #uuid "9f228826-f550-52dc-83d1-70ff2e48a178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261255} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc4bc7-93b1-5f30-8801-7d45f0f67ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261649} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26827a-6b30-5805-9ec2-35c779e0f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc4bc7-93b1-5f30-8801-7d45f0f67ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a2ac70-d4cf-5928-8804-e1eb884d3787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bab01488-68e9-5dde-9b92-c15b0288a3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127072" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17551007-968c-50bb-8713-508a56887419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415201} {:answer/id #uuid "21edad9a-788d-531c-a881-1f0e31295a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415203} {:answer/id #uuid "89ae9690-9dcf-58c2-8b46-641ef0e6072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415229} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4ab0a1-3455-57e5-b1fe-fa7e38dc4456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415233} {:answer/id #uuid "b159a7f5-1313-51eb-887e-62e29469c644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ae9690-9dcf-58c2-8b46-641ef0e6072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415229} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b140e5-29aa-510b-86ab-1d425008b9cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f45f3fc-c4df-53d5-b37f-c7ba8162617a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98951" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xyz+{y}^{2}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,-1)$ to $(2,1,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,-1)$ and $B(2,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,1)-f(1,0,-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+{1}^{2})-(1\\cdot 0\\cdot (-1)+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C} \\left(e^{-x^2}-y^3\\right) \\mathrm{d}x + ( e^{-y^2}+x^3) \\: \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}-y^3, \\qquad\n\nQ(x,y) = e^{-y^2}+x^3,\n$$\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  P}{\\partial  y}} = - 3 {y}^{2} , \\hspace{2em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial  x}} = 3 {x}^{2} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\underset{C}{∮} ( {e}^{- {x}^{2}} - {y}^{3} ) \\mathrm{d} x + ( {e}^{- {y}^{2}} + {x}^{3} ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\mathrm{d} y & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } ( \\frac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial  x} - \\frac{\\partial  P}{\\partial  y} ) \\text{d} A \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } ( 3 {x}^{2} - ( - 3 {y}^{2} ) ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{d} A \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } 3 ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{d} A .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\underset{D}{\\iint } 3 ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} )  \\text{d} A &  = \\underset{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}{\\iint } 3 ( {r}^{2} {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {r}^{2} {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  )  r  \\text{d} r \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{2} 3 {r}^{3} ( {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  )  \\text{d} r  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } {\\int }_{0}^{2} 3 {r}^{3}  \\text{d} r  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } {[ \\frac{3 {r}^{4}}{4} ]}_{0}^{2}  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi } 12  \\text{d} \\theta  \\\\  &  = 12 [ \\theta  {]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  &  = 12 \\pi \\end{array}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n=\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\! \\int_{0}^2 \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A}}} \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afcc73d-a8a6-5615-9d7c-fa0873dda7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}2r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242139} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b68d-147e-555d-9b7c-88c63841a64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2aeae-ff81-519e-b9b7-d9f8716c6540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242523} {:answer/id #uuid "bc85f820-b7f6-5517-a066-986ad9b00f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242832} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a5a0d5-6ff6-5128-b57e-53fc5760b7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b68d-147e-555d-9b7c-88c63841a64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371} :answer-field/id #uuid "488d900f-29ce-5ad3-9267-3a76fc467aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6e3c388-6148-5c9d-8b7f-1f6ea60657b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108544" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0964ff8219d84181163537f5660fe2b504668b1e97e3461cc8cb43d18fc6091.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/de/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f2c80d-9cac-5756-9929-d92d4f8f3e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416075} {:answer/id #uuid "168b8872-373b-550c-a253-e7487bbea803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416076} {:answer/id #uuid "40901d5c-3eac-5579-8462-7e9d79976125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416080} {:answer/id #uuid "6f76d14c-7e3b-59e6-b71d-5d52071de026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416085} {:answer/id #uuid "7691659c-2eed-5772-8397-8d23f26bb55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f76d14c-7e3b-59e6-b71d-5d52071de026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416085} :answer-field/id #uuid "064abd0a-e4c9-5ad6-9b82-ef5bf4dea054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac600244-dbff-596b-9fbb-094ec0801e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109119" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,4-x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,Q(x,y)=4-x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6763f71a-2f1e-5e1a-b5b0-80171e8077f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537433} {:answer/id #uuid "da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537665} {:answer/id #uuid "dce15c84-2fee-5b91-8d2e-70aabd4925cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537672} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bae659-b917-5899-868d-3dd4b4436e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537702} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7dfd07-1378-5021-b284-61af5aa9f83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537665} :answer-field/id #uuid "9608a74f-b086-5f31-b192-ae95683f2fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77d34a7-9f25-5206-895c-5f4fa0d1f689" :question/math-academy-id "q-125716" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨x{y}^{2},\\,x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d831c7fb805d16ec7a2dc2462813ea79b78d888ed2121330ed5d823b590d3c4f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r-{r}^{3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21230e40-c778-5976-8975-77b9141fa55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539545} {:answer/id #uuid "82f5e165-d4c5-5f87-9f4c-f1a35e90641d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539695} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7d42e8-02bc-575f-bdc0-3241bc078dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539711} {:answer/id #uuid "c56b99d4-e511-50b9-9301-30b784d9db25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539819} {:answer/id #uuid "eae586ab-e6cd-5a7e-8c86-9db144eadecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56b99d4-e511-50b9-9301-30b784d9db25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539819} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f67038-add1-5bf7-87fe-fead30bf61e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "552ffe88-dfcd-57ba-97f8-f7bf0a708429" :question/math-academy-id "q-125717" :question/problem "![](images/66/66ab1a3e2e7244a9b3e5fc9bda814736194c28dd9438fbb97086e99be4633261.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/70/709d108b2b992d1714c7c0b3bef26f52e965c2467f38d05103cd45f4f9bc821b.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{y}^{2}-(-4{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378b0460-32af-5257-b39e-eb191e6b7ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271} {:answer/id #uuid "45df6382-7cbc-520f-a94b-e0654045f8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257393} {:answer/id #uuid "80f5da35-1f8d-5b8e-98bc-a87edfbce6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257928} {:answer/id #uuid "d9166bd6-4167-5918-9672-1af0c9421726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258683} {:answer/id #uuid "e9823d2d-a2cd-57c0-bf4a-ee850ecc8da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "378b0460-32af-5257-b39e-eb191e6b7ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257271} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca565c59-abfb-57f2-916c-26752c3de0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20585d4c-063f-50c6-8591-a785c62137ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-125719" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95640d76-c966-5192-a081-34cc37eff21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416089} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfeea07-4dc9-5945-8f32-4ba04d4c39db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0c1485-b37d-59e9-8042-95dc6071d26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416094} {:answer/id #uuid "a7529453-a85e-5454-aaea-6763faa38e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416096} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f2f158-1952-54d5-b338-cc98999f91b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95640d76-c966-5192-a081-34cc37eff21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416089} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8487d6d-ade8-5e6b-aa72-6dcb7d480679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdd86cce-9e5b-5a18-9af7-54a7f6efd03e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41469" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/45/459a51f3c408a9c647b3be6b978267a6ce724e747118fbe897275c370d2bb6d6.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right)=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2605ce5a-8cc3-5767-bd42-b4df2fc57fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177846} {:answer/id #uuid "090f3e9e-10f1-5380-9922-651ecfe6ea3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551683} {:answer/id #uuid "6d43715d-8bf7-5ec2-8e40-279a007829e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551922} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c4f06f-dc17-5580-804b-5e960683c34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552096} {:answer/id #uuid "d72ed648-66b7-5576-93c4-769d0caa8136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2605ce5a-8cc3-5767-bd42-b4df2fc57fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25cf8ea-2732-5072-883d-71745e726402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6daea5f-9d1c-5bf1-b6af-12c3102e71d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126717" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-x=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1856b101-7baf-59fd-834b-982658a922fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260088} {:answer/id #uuid "1f19942f-78b6-5bf3-b50c-884e00feb139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260143} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3a8518-8872-5062-8257-1de19938dc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260514} {:answer/id #uuid "a5164cfa-c311-554e-b1f3-9bea6aceb7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261310} {:answer/id #uuid "d560e271-d185-5a8f-b214-1548caab5e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5164cfa-c311-554e-b1f3-9bea6aceb7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261310} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60a314a-1658-5f43-adea-650d5ae0b480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1687af9-fdc5-568f-9b8e-74b394a32f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126719" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+5\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{cos}^{2}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153220d6-7689-5056-8b5b-6d10695accb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260060} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef2d5d-86ef-5bc0-a9ec-82673d7b7699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260393} {:answer/id #uuid "7523559d-4e43-59f2-bd01-34c48fcecddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260899} {:answer/id #uuid "9f78043b-d21c-5406-8dbe-ff8fab366365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261259} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe74df2-842d-5ac2-840e-8b0249def4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "153220d6-7689-5056-8b5b-6d10695accb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260060} :answer-field/id #uuid "e164db38-546a-5334-8c07-335889e10d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0b1aae8-7058-5a37-a641-ca7e8583e806" :question/math-academy-id "q-126940" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/e9/e90c36e34c6ab51f15707685a22c8bd2c749b9857ca24c59631f09dfbda495c6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t)\\cdot (2t)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b1e968e-8930-58be-ab71-3bd0f25e502c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260814} {:answer/id #uuid "8333823b-23fc-55f7-8631-0c3b55eaeefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261014} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d2cd70-36ee-5747-b7fa-0641fc3c4792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261359} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8eec96-250f-548c-8bb5-2b5e1f480ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261676} {:answer/id #uuid "fb24cd87-3b97-54a1-84bc-1c02515203f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc8eec96-250f-548c-8bb5-2b5e1f480ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261676} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2711ee6-8fbc-5466-bb32-b1817aaad6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72bfedb9-24a7-5361-9689-9e032cb56805" :question/math-academy-id "q-126943" :question/problem "![](images/27/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\,i-4\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2.)$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/b7/b7ac1ce4a79528f5660b8f898739da72489ebe8e8fc156020efae77a103fbec4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2)\\cdot (1)-(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfab73e-541e-5dea-a884-264b4bd8ec97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177913} {:answer/id #uuid "1d03d1d4-83e8-5434-ba88-654e772f6034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539535} {:answer/id #uuid "2bedcbb9-3a20-535c-92e0-bda037bb0531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539560} {:answer/id #uuid "94ef6336-a885-5e0f-9339-fb9e597d51dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539726} {:answer/id #uuid "c61c8d76-f473-524c-b92c-3be06cc40c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d03d1d4-83e8-5434-ba88-654e772f6034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539535} :answer-field/id #uuid "dab28699-4fc7-5cb2-8f84-87f7666ba43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4edd3b9a-070a-593a-8f84-1b14657d2c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-127055" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x+y=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y-x=3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd04950-8c76-5e5c-bd3b-57f33b66e8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177916} {:answer/id #uuid "3d33d012-f495-55e4-a694-7649d9356119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177917} {:answer/id #uuid "470219b9-52ad-5f5d-85b1-f6487d2469f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177918} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3d558e-c45c-5b6d-ba67-dbe452d8b64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177919} {:answer/id #uuid "77a8d59a-d4b3-536c-9bf3-7c9b158878e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f3d558e-c45c-5b6d-ba67-dbe452d8b64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6d5bae-0477-56a7-a550-3209e91d41b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14039085-c7d5-567d-835b-0c3eaccf0c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-127141" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+xy\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =xy=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+18{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }26{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}26{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
